\begin_layout Plain Layout

\series bold
Exercise 7.1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Find 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi\left(t\right)}$
\end_inset

 for a QCA cell prepared in state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi\left(0\right)}=1$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma=50\;\text{meV}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+html:
 <details><summary>Show/hide solution ...</summary>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Assuming a constant Hamiltonian,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

,
 e will use the solution of Equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:TDSE-Solution-Const-H-compact"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 It can be shown that for 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}=-\gamma\mathbf{X}$
\end_inset

,
 the time evolution operator is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\hat{U}\left(t\right) & \stackrel{\left(\ref{eq:TimeEvolutionOperator}\right)}{=}\exp\left(-i\gamma\mathbf{X}t\right)=\cos\left(\frac{\gamma}{\hbar}t\right)\mathbf{I}-i\sin\left(\frac{\gamma}{\hbar}t\right)\hat{\sigma}_{x}.\\
 & =\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\cos\left(\frac{\gamma}{\hbar}t\right) & -i\sin\left(\frac{\gamma}{\hbar}t\right)\\
-i\sin\left(\frac{\gamma}{\hbar}t\right) & \cos\left(\frac{\gamma}{\hbar}t\right)
\end{array}\right]
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Now,
 we can easily find
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\ket{\psi\left(t\right)} & =\hat{U}\left(t\right)\ket{\psi\left(0\right)}\\
 & =\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\cos\left(\frac{\gamma}{\hbar}t\right) & -i\sin\left(\frac{\gamma}{\hbar}t\right)\\
-i\sin\left(\frac{\gamma}{\hbar}t\right) & \cos\left(\frac{\gamma}{\hbar}t\right)
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
0\\
1
\end{array}\right]\\
 & =\left[\begin{array}{c}
-i\sin\left(\frac{\gamma}{\hbar}t\right)\\
\cos\left(\frac{\gamma}{\hbar}t\right)
\end{array}\right]\\
 & =-i\sin\left(\frac{\gamma}{\hbar}t\right)\ket{0}+\cos\left(\frac{\gamma}{\hbar}t\right)\ket{1}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Is this reasonable?
 A quick check at 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0$
\end_inset

 indeed confirms that 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi\left(0\right)}=\ket{1}$
\end_inset

.
 We also note that 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi\left(t\right)}$
\end_inset

 is periodic with a period,
 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{0}$
\end_inset

,
 that can be determined by setting
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\gamma}{\hbar}T_{0}=2\pi,
\]

\end_inset

which yields 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
T_{0}=\frac{2\pi\hbar}{\gamma}
\]

\end_inset

At 
\begin_inset Formula $t=T_{0}/4=\pi\hbar/2\gamma$
\end_inset

,
 we find
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\ket{\psi\left(\frac{\pi\hbar}{2\gamma}\right)} & =-i\sin\left(\frac{\gamma}{\hbar}\cdot\frac{\pi\hbar}{2\gamma}\right)\ket{0}+\cos\left(\frac{\gamma}{\hbar}\cdot\frac{\pi\hbar}{2\gamma}\right)\ket{1}\\
 & =-i\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)\ket{0}+\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)\ket{1}\\
 & =-i\ket{0}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

We see that the system will oscillate between the computational basis states in what is called a Rabi oscillation.
\end_layout

