\begin_layout Itemize
We see the QCA cell oscillate between 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{1}$
\end_inset

 with period 
\begin_inset Formula $T=\pi\hbar/\gamma$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This oscillation is known as Rabi oscillation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is called the Rabi oscillation period
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This is the lower limit for electron transfer times in our QCA cell
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This provides a physically relevant time scale
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
It is often useful to scale plots to a physically meaningful scale 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This helps us avoid thinking about exact numbers,
 but rather more physically signficant quantities
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We also see that total energy is conserved
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The QCA cell in isolation is a closed system:
 it can neither gain nor loose energy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Why is the energy zero and not something else?
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Recall that the diagonal elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 are potential energies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We can pick the zero of potential energy to be anything
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In this case,
 we chose 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 as the average energy of states 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{1}$
\end_inset

 way back in Equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:ChooseQCAZero"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 and there is no bias (
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta=0$
\end_inset

) for the isolated cell
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Thus,
 both 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{1}$
\end_inset

 have an energy of 0
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We started with 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left(0\right)=0$
\end_inset

,
 and so our closed system is fixed
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
